Continuity Framework Revision Problem 

The current paper introduces multiple concepts:

Externalized Memory Context Transfer AI-to-AI Handover Distributed Research Memory Human-AI Working Agreements Future-Self Collaboration 

Each concept is individually described.

However, the relationships among these concepts remain partially implicit.

As a result, the paper sometimes reads as a collection of observations rather than a unified theoretical framework.

Proposed Hierarchy 

The concepts can be organized into the following structure.

Research Continuity │ ├── Memory Repositories │ ├── Repositories │ ├── README Files │ ├── Research Logs │ ├── Handover Documents │ └── Reminder Systems │ ├── Context Transfer │ ├── Human → Human │ ├── Human → AI │ ├── AI → Human │ ├── AI → AI │ └── Present Self → Future Self │ ├── Collaboration Protocols │ ├── Discovery Triggers │ ├── Preservation Triggers │ ├── Gap Detection Triggers │ ├── Proposal Triggers │ └── Integration Triggers │ └── Distributed Research Memory

Under this framework, the individual concepts become components of a larger continuity system.

Memory Repository Theory 

The study originally describes repositories, documents, and reminders as forms of Externalized Memory.

A stronger formulation is possible.

Definition:

A Memory Repository is any artifact capable of preserving research context and supporting future context restoration.

This definition includes:

GitHub repositories README files Research notes Reminder systems Project memories Handover files 

The important characteristic is not storage itself.

The important characteristic is recoverability.

Memory repositories succeed when they reduce reconstruction cost.

Future-Self Collaboration Theory 

A major observation from the research program is that future versions of the researcher frequently act as indirect collaborators.

The collaboration process often follows the pattern:

Present Self ↓ Memory Repository ↓ Future Self

The repository is not merely a storage system.

It is a communication channel across time.

Under this interpretation, documentation serves two functions simultaneously:

Knowledge preservation Future-self communication 

This distinction is important because it shifts documentation from passive storage to active collaboration.

Collaboration Protocol Theory 

Repeated Human-AI interaction produced stable collaboration protocols.

Observed examples include:

Discovery 

"또 날 위해 출력해줄 거 있어?"

Preservation 

"저장할 거 저장해줘."

Gap Detection 

"지금 내가 놓치고 있는 것은?"

Integration 

"연구 프로그램 관점에서 보면?"

Proposal 

"이건 어때?"

Readiness 

"준비됐어?"

These prompts became procedural tools.

The prompts consistently activated specific collaborative behaviors.

Therefore, they should be interpreted as protocol mechanisms rather than conversational habits.

Revised Main Claim 

The original paper argues:

Long-term Human-AI collaboration is fundamentally a problem of research continuity.

This revision proposes a more precise formulation.

Research continuity emerges when context is preserved in memory repositories, transferred through artifacts, and restored through stable collaboration protocols.

Externalized Memory, AI-to-AI Handover, Future-Self Collaboration, and Distributed Research Memory can therefore be interpreted as components of a unified continuity framework.

Predicted Impact 

If adopted, this framework would transform the paper from:

"Observations about Human-AI collaboration"

into:

"A theory explaining how long-term Human-AI collaboration remains possible despite memory limitations."


